The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```java
for (String serviceConfigType : stack.getConfigurationTypes(service)) {
  if (mapClusterConfigurations.containsKey(serviceConfigType)) {
    blueprintConfigRequest.addConfigElement(serviceConfigType,
      mapClusterConfigurations.get(serviceConfigType),
      mapClusterAttributes.get(serviceConfigType));
  }
}
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because the loop iterates over all configuration types for each service, and if a configuration type has already been processed, it can still be added again due to the lack of a mechanism to track which configuration types have already been encountered. This leads to the same configuration type being applied multiple times, resulting in inconsistencies.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```java
Collection<String> encounteredConfigTypes = new HashSet<String>();
if (encounteredConfigTypes.add(serviceConfigType)) {
  if (mapClusterConfigurations.containsKey(serviceConfigType)) {
    blueprintConfigRequest.addConfigElement(serviceConfigType,
      mapClusterConfigurations.get(serviceConfigType),
      mapClusterAttributes.get(serviceConfigType));
  }
}
```
Reason:
The fix introduces a `HashSet` called `encounteredConfigTypes` to track configuration types that have already been added. The use of `add()` method ensures that a configuration type is only added if it has not been seen before, preventing duplicate application and thereby ensuring the configuration is set on the cluster no more than once.

